/*@
\chapter{Dominator Tree}
\label{ir_dom}

Several properties of the control flow graph of a function, and the
basic blocks (\autoref{ir:block}) that it contains, are informed by the
concept of dominance.

If the block [[x]] appears on every control-flow path from the function
entry to the block [[y]], then [[x]] \textit{dominates} [[y]]. If, in
addition, [[x != y]], then [[x]] \textit{strictly dominates} [[y]].
[[x]] is the \textit{immediate dominator} of [[y]] if [[x]] strictly
dominates [[y]], and every other dominator of [[y]] dominates [[x]]
\citep{tarjan79,cytron91}.

The \textit{dominator tree} of a function is rooted at the [[.entry]]
block, and every other block [[y]] has its immediate dominator as its
parent in the tree \citep{cytron91}.

The \textit{dominance frontier} of a block [[x]] is the set of all
blocks [[y]] such that [[x]] dominates some predecessor of [[y]] but
doesn't strictly dominate [[y]] \citep{cytron91}.

==ir_dom.c*/
//includes//
//intersect//
